{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, when Isaac was old, and his eyes were dim from seeing.</b> He did not use the expression 'come into days,' because the expression 'come into days' implies that the light of his mind was growing ever stronger, in the manner of elderly Torah scholars, whose intellectual light increases all the more at the time of their old age, as though they are coming from the nights into the days, as explained above in the portion of חַיֵּי שָׂרָה on the verse ״וְאַבְרָהָם זָקֵן בָּא בַּיָּמִים״ (Genesis 24:1). Therefore he says here concerning Isaac, that even at the time of his old age the eye of his mind grew dim from seeing through the deceits of Esau, for he was mistaken about him and supposed that he was righteous, for he did not perceive his craftiness. Therefore it is not said 'come into days,' for on the contrary, he walked in darkness and there was no glow to see the appearances of truth in the matters of Esau his son's conduct." "And perhaps on this Rashi explained, 'They grew dim from the smoke of these' — for could it enter your mind that Isaac saw them offering incense to idolatry and did not rebuke them? For Abraham was scrupulous not to allow idolatry, and Isaac certainly would have been careful about idolatry like him. Rather, Isaac surely supposed that they were offering incense to Heaven. Therefore it says immediately after ״וַתִּהְיֶיןָ מֹרַת רוּחַ לְיִצְחָק וּלְרִבְקָה״ (Genesis 26:35), ״וַיְהִי כִּי זָקֵן יִצְחָק וַתִּכְהֶיןָ עֵינָיו״ (Genesis 27:1) — that their spirit was not at ease because of their deeds, for even though they said that they were offering incense to Heaven, nevertheless their spirit was not at ease because of their deeds, for they suspected them that perhaps they were profaning the offering by means of some alien thought. And what is written 'מֹרַת רוּחַ' — it refers to the spirit of Isaac and Rebekah, or it refers to those who offer incense, for perhaps in the spirit within them they are rebellious, and who knows what is in their heart. Therefore he said ״וַתִּכְהֶיןָ עֵינָיו מֵרְאֹת״, for the eyes of his mind did not see through their stratagems and through the stratagems of Esau his son. This is what he said 'בַּעֲשָׁנָן שֶׁל אֵלּוּ,' meaning that he was mistaken in his mind regarding the smoke of these." "And according to its plain sense, one may answer what it says ״מֵרְאֹת״, which appears superfluous — but surely the Holy One, blessed be He, caused the dimming of his eyes for this reason, so that he would not see his daughters-in-law offering incense to idolatry and would not be distressed by this. This is what they said: 'בַּעֲשָׁנָן שֶׁל אֵלּוּ' — on account of their smoke, so that he would not see this and be distressed. And what Rashi explained differently — in order that Jacob should take the blessings — accordingly ״מֵרְאֹת״ is causal, and he meant to say that for this reason his eyes grew dim, so that he would not see who is Esau and who is Jacob, and by means of this cause Jacob would take the blessings. Therefore ״מֵרְאֹת״ is stated, so that he would not see and recognize his sons at a glance."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:31.830739590Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}